@flexiarg or3/openness-without-exposure
@title Openness Without Exposure
@audience qualitative researchers, OR coordinators, ethics leads, REF leads
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🪟/境 🛡️/护]
@see or3/consent-bounded-analysis, or3/ask-first-then-bring-the-expert

! conclusion: Openness Without Exposure

  + context: Open research is being promoted to researchers whose data cannot responsibly be published.

  + IF:
    Your colleagues work with rich qualitative material — interviews, ethnography, case work — and open research is arriving as an expectation, possibly as an assessment criterion.

  + HOWEVER:
    The message lands as a demand to expose informants, and the objection is correct: a transcript can be anonymised, but rich context re-identifies people, and the richer the account the worse the problem. Where openness starts to look like a quality metric, researchers who already feel disadvantaged in assessment exercises reasonably conclude that the disadvantage is deepening — and disengage entirely, under the heading "this isn't for me."

  + THEN:
    Say plainly and early that not publishing transcripts is a legitimate open-research position, not a failure to comply. Then move the question from what you can release to what context you can supply alongside publications: reflexivity statements, decision trails, coding frameworks, analytic memos, and pre-registration adapted to qualitative work — for which community-developed templates already exist. Bring in practitioners from the qualitative tradition to make the case, rather than arguing it from a quantitative default.

  + BECAUSE:
    Openness is about making reasoning inspectable, and for qualitative work the reasoning — not the raw material — is what carries the inferential weight. Framed this way it becomes a method rather than a threat, and researchers report the extra space for communicating reflexivity as genuinely useful. Refusing the false choice is also what keeps them in the conversation at all.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Add "you do not have to post your transcripts" to the first slide of any open research session with a qualitative audience.]
    next[Point colleagues at the community-developed qualitative pre-registration template (Haven et al. 2020, doi:10.1177/1609406920976417).]
